gory" href="./Category:Articles_with_criticism_or_controversy_sections_from_February_2024"/><link rel="mw:PageProp/Category" href="./Category:All_articles_with_criticism_or_controversy_sections"/></span>

<section data-mw-section-id="15" id="mwBIM"><h3 data-mw-wikitext="" id="Pay_for_PhD_students">Pay for PhD students</h3>
<p id="mwBIQ">In 2008, the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./General_Court_(European_Union)" title="General Court (European Union)" id="mwBIU">European General Court</a> ruled in a case brought by a PhD student against the Max Planck Society that "a researcher preparing a doctoral thesis on the basis of a grant contract concluded with the Max-Planck-Gesellschaft zur Förderung der Wissenschaften eV, must be regarded as a worker within the meaning of Article 39 EC only if his activities are performed for a certain period of time under the direction of an institute forming part of that association and if, in return for those activities, he receives remuneration".<sup about="#mwt204" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-57" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-57"}}'><a href="./Max_Planck_Society#cite_note-57" id="mwBIY"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBIc"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBIg">[</span>57<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBIk">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwBIo">In 2012, the Max Planck Society was at the centre of a controversy about some PhD students not being given <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Employment_contracts" title="Employment contracts" class="mw-redirect" id="mwBIs">employment contracts</a>. Of the 5,300 students who at the time wrote their PhD thesis at the 80 Max Planck Institutes 2,000 had an employment contract. The remaining 3,300 received grants of between 1,000 and 1,365 Euro.<sup about="#mwt207" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-58" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-58"}}'><a href="./Max_Planck_Society#cite_note-58" id="mwBIw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBI0"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBI4">[</span>58<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBI8">]</span></span></a></sup> According to a 2011 statement by the Max Planck Society "As you embark on a PhD, you are still anything but a <i id="mwBJA">proper</i> scientist; it's during the process itself that you become a <i id="mwBJE">proper</i> scientist... a PhD is <i id="mwBJI">an apprenticeship in the lab</i>, and as such it is usually not paid like a <i id="mwBJM">proper</i> job – and this is, by and large, the practice at all research institutions and universities".<sup about="#mwt210" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-59" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-59"}}'><a href="./Max_Planck_Society#cite_note-59" id="mwBJQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBJU"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBJY">[</span>59<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBJc">]</span></span></a></sup> The allegation of wage dumping for young scientists was discussed during the passing of the 2012 "Wissenschaftsfreiheitsgesetz" (Scientific Freedom Law) in the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./German_Parliament" title="German Parliament" class="mw-redirect" id="mwBJg">German Parliament</a>.<sup about="#mwt213" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-60" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-60"}}'><a href="./Max_Planck_Society#cite_note-60" id="mwBJk"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBJo"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBJs">[</span>60<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBJw">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section><section data-mw-section-id="16" id="mwBJ0"><h3 data-mw-wikitext="" id="Freedom_of_expression">Freedom of expression</h3>
<p id="mwBJ4">In February 2024, the Max Planck Society faced widespread criticism for terminating the employment of Lebanese-Australian professor <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Ghassan_Hage" title="Ghassan Hage" id="mwBJ8">Ghassan Hage</a> from the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Max_Planck_Institute_for_Social_Anthropology" title="Max Planck Institute for Social Anthropology" id="mwBKA">Max Planck Institute for Social Anthropology</a>, citing his social media posts on the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Israeli–Palestinian_conflict" title="Israeli–Palestinian conflict" id="mwBKE">Israeli–Palestinian conflict</a> as incompatible with the society's core values.<sup about="#mwt216" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-61" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-61"}}'><a href="./Max_Planck_Society#cite_note-61" id="mwBKI"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBKM"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBKQ">[</span>61<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBKU">]</span></span></a></sup> This decision was publicly condemned by numerous scholars and academic organizations, who argued it infringed on Hage's <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Freedom_of_expression" title="Freedom of expression" class="mw-redirect" id="mwBKY">freedom of expression</a>. German newspaper <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Welt_am_Sonntag" title="Welt am Sonntag" id="mwBKc">Welt am Sonntag</a> initially reported on Hage's posts.<sup about="#mwt219" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-62" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-62"}}'><a href="./Max_Planck_Society#cite_note-62" id="mwBKg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBKk"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBKo">[</span>62<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBKs">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt222" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-63" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-63"}}'><a href="./Max_Planck_Society#cite_note-63" id="mwBKw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBK0"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBK4">[</span>63<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBK8">]</span></span></a></sup> Following the dismissal, global academic communities, including Israeli scholars,<sup about="#mwt225" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-64" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-64"}}'><a href="./Max_Planck_Society#cite_note-64" id="mwBLA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBLE"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBLI">[</span>64<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBLM">]</span></span></a></sup> the German Association of Social and Cultural Anthropology,<sup about="#mwt228" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-65" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-65"}}'><a href="./Max_Planck_Society#cite_note-65" id="mwBLQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBLU"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBLY">[</span>65<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBLc">]</span></span></a></sup> the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./British_Society_for_Middle_Eastern_Studies" title="British Society for Middle Eastern Studies" id="mwBLg">British Society for Middle Eastern Studies</a>,<sup about="#mwt231" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-66" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-66"}}'><a href="./Max_Planck_Society#cite_note-66" id="mwBLk"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBLo"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBLs">[</span>66<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBLw">]</span></span></a></sup> the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./European_Association_of_Social_Anthropologists" title="European Association of Social Anthropologists" id="mwBL0">European Association of Social Anthropologists</a>,<sup about="#mwt234" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-67" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-67"}}'><a href="./Max_Planck_Society#cite_note-67" id="mwBL4"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBL8"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBMA">[</span>67<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBME">]</span></span></a></sup> the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./American_Anthropological_Association" title="American Anthropological Association" id="mwBMI">American Anthropological Association</a>,<sup about="#mwt237" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-68" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-68"}}'><a href="./Max_Planck_Society#cite_note-68" id="mwBMM"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBMQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBMU">[</span>68<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBMY">]</span></span></a></sup> the Council for Humanities, Arts and Sciences and the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Australian_Anthropological_Society" title="Australian Anthropological Society" id="mwBMc">Australian Anthropological Society</a>,<sup about="#mwt240" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-69" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-69"}}'><a href="./Max_Planck_Society#cite_note-69" id="mwBMg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBMk"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBMo">[</span>69<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBMs">]</span></span></a></sup> the Canadian Anthropology Society,<sup about="#mwt243" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-70" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-70"}}'><a href="./Max_Planck_Society#cite_note-70" id="mwBMw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBM0"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBM4">[</span>70<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBM8">]</span></span></a></sup> a Japanese group of scholars,<sup about="#mwt246" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-71" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-71"}}'><a href="./Max_Planck_Society#cite_note-71" id="mwBNA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBNE"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBNI">[</span>71<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBNM">]</span></span></a></sup> the Australian Sociological Association,<sup about="#mwt249" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-72" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-72"}}'><a href="./Max_Planck_Society#cite_note-72" id="mwBNQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBNU"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBNY">[</span>72<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBNc">]</span></span></a></sup> rallied in support of Hage, extensively citing Hage's own intellectual work, urging the society to reverse its decision. The Max Planck Society and the President Patrick Cramer have not yet respond to these letters, as of July 2024.<sup about="#mwt254" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-73" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-73"}}'><a href="./Max_Planck_Society#cite_note-73" id="mwBNg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBNk"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBNo">[</span>73<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBNs">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt257" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-74" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-74"}}'><a href="./Max_Planck_Society#cite_note-74" id="mwBNw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBN0"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBN4">[</span>74<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBN8">]</span></span></a></sup> The Max Planck Society has made public statements expressing support for the state of Israel in the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Gaza_war" title="Gaza war" id="mwBOA">Gaza war</a>.<sup about="#mwt260" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-75" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-75"}}'><a href="./Max_Planck_Society#cite_note-75" id="mwBOE"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBOI"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBOM">[</span>75<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBOQ">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt263" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-76" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-76"}}'><a href="./Max_Planck_Society#cite_note-76" id="mwBOU"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBOY"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBOc">[</span>76<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBOg">]</span></span></a></sup> Hage filed a legal challenge contesting his dismissal. In December 2024, the Labour Court Halle rejected the dismissal claim of Ghassan Hage.<sup about="#mwt264" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-77" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-77"}}'><a href="./Max_Planck_Society#cite_note-77" id="mwBOk"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBOo"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBOs">[</span>77<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBOw">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section><section data-mw-section-id="17" id="mwBO0"><h3 data-mw-wikitext="" id="Allegations_of_misconduct">Allegations of misconduct</h3>
<p id="mwBO4">Since at least 2018, there have been numerous accusations of bullying and harassment by senior researchers and directors at the Max Planck Society. In 2018, two high-profile cases of bullying were made public. Researchers at the Max Planck Institute for Astrophysics (MPA) in Garching accused director <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Guinevere_Kauffmann" title="Guinevere Kauffmann" id="mwBO8">Guinevere Kauffmann</a> of insulting and bullying students and making racist remarks.<sup about="#mwt267" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-78" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-78"}}'><a href="./Max_Planck_Society#cite_note-78" id="mwBPA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBPE"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBPI">[</span>78<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBPM">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt270" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-79" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-79"}}'><a href="./Max_Planck_Society#cite_note-79" id="mwBPQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBPU"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBPY">[</span>79<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBPc">]</span></span></a></sup> Researchers at the Max Planck Institute for Human Cognitive and Brain Sciences also accused director <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Tania_Singer" title="Tania Singer" id="mwBPg">Tania Singer</a> of bullying and intimidation.<sup about="#mwt273" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-80" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-80"}}'><a href="./Max_Planck_Society#cite_note-80" id="mwBPk"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBPo"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBPs">[</span>80<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBPw">]</span></span></a></sup> Singer resigned later that year, and Kauffman remains a director at MPA as of July 2026.<sup about="#mwt276" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-81" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-81"}}'><a href="./Max_Planck_Society#cite_note-81" id="mwBP0"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBP4"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBP8">[</span>81<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBQA">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwBQE">In 2021, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Nicole_Boivin" title="Nicole Boivin" id="mwBQI">Nicole Boivin</a>, a director of the Max Planck Institute for the Science of Human History (renamed the Max Planck Institute for Geoanthropology in 2022), was removed after an internal investigation by the Max Planck Society reportedly determined that she had bullied junior researchers and plagiarized their work, along with other accusations. In December 2021, a court ruling reinstated her as a director. However in April 2022, she was removed following a vote by a governing board of the Max Planck Society.<sup about="#mwt279" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-82" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-82"}}'><a href="./Max_Planck_Society#cite_note-82" id="mwBQM"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBQQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBQU">[</span>82<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBQY">]</span></span></a></sup> However, as of April 2025, she is still employed as a Research Group Leader.<sup about="#mwt282" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-83" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-83"}}'><a href="./Max_Planck_Society#cite_note-83" id="mwBQc"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBQg"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBQk">[</span>83<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBQo">]</span></span></a></sup> The same year, ecologist <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Ian_T._Baldwin" title="Ian T. Baldwin" id="mwBQs">Ian Baldwin</a>, a director at the Max Planck Institute for Chemical Ecology, was accused of harassing doctoral candidates and postdoctoral researchers.<sup about="#mwt285" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-84" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-84"}}'><a href="./Max_Planck_Society#cite_note-84" id="mwBQw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBQ0"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBQ4">[</span>84<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBQ8">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwBRA">In March 2025, a joint investigation between <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Deutsche_Welle" title="Deutsche Welle" id="mwBRE">Deutsche Welle</a> and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Der_Spiegel" title="Der Spiegel" id="mwBRI">Der Spiegel</a> claimed there was "a systemic failure to hold abusive staff members or their institutes accountable" within the institutes they investigated. Interviews with around 30 scientists, many recruited internationally, revealed that more than half experienced or witnessed misconduct by senior staff, particularly directors and group leaders. Women and people of color were identified as being at higher risk of such abuse. Many of those interviewed within the report wished to remain anonymous to avoid retaliation. Specific allegations of misconduct against <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Jan-Michael_Rost" title="Jan-Michael Rost" class="mw-redirect" id="mwBRM">Jan-Michael Rost</a>, director of the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Max_Planck_Institute_for_the_Physics_of_Complex_Systems" title="Max Planck Institute for the Physics of Complex Systems" id="mwBRQ">Max Planck Institute for the Physics of Complex Systems</a> in <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Dresden" title="Dresden" id="mwBRU">Dresden</a>, were highlighted within the report.<sup about="#mwt288" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-85" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-85"}}'><a href="./Max_Planck_Society#cite_note-85" id="mwBRY"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBRc"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBRg">[</span>85<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBRk">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt291" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-86" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-86"}}'><a href="./Max_Planck_Society#cite_note-86" id="mwBRo"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBRs"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBRw">[</span>86<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBR0">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section></section><section data-mw-section-id="18" id="mwBR4"><h2 data-mw-wikitext="" id="Nobel_Laureates">Nobel Laureates</h2>

